The code here is obviously wrong due to checking `y` ⊆ `x` only.  I guess no one have been using it.
```C#
var x = new HashSet<string>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) { "foo", "bar" };
var y = new HashSet<string>(StringComparer.Ordinal) { "foo" };
var comparer = HashSet<string>.CreateSetComparer();
Console.WriteLine(comparer.Equals(x, y)); // True (incorrect)
```

BTW, I'm not sure whether we test that binary serialization (boo) works for both secure and non secure string comparers. I do see a dictionary test with a comment asserting that it should be invisible:
```c#
        [Fact]
        public void ComparerSerialization()
        {
            // Strings switch between randomized and non-randomized comparers,
            // however this should never be observable externally.
            TestComparerSerialization(EqualityComparer<string>.Default);
```
Anyway - if we have such a test, it should possibly exist for both.
